      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ideas or effort, but because roadmaps are often treated as promises rather than as hypotheses. A roadmap should communicate a direction: the problems a company intends to solve, the outcomes it wants to create, and the sequence in which it will learn. Instead, many organizations turn roadmaps into detailed feature calendars. Once dates, features, and commitments are published, changing course can look like failure—even when customer evidence says it is necessary.\n\nOne major cause is weak customer understanding. Teams frequently build roadmaps around internal opinions, executive requests, competitor features, or sales escalations. These inputs can be useful, but they are not substitutes for evidence. A customer asking for a feature may be describing a symptom rather than the underlying problem. Without research, usage data, and direct observation, teams risk delivering polished solutions to problems that are unimportant, poorly understood, or limited to a small group of users.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they focus on outputs rather than outcomes. “Launch reporting dashboard in Q3” is an output. “Help account managers identify at-risk customers earlier” is an outcome. Output-based roadmaps encourage teams to measure success by whether they shipped on time. Outcome-based roadmaps ask whether the shipped work improved retention, adoption, revenue, efficiency, or customer satisfaction. A product can be delivered perfectly and still fail commercially.\n\nAnother problem is the illusion of certainty. Product development involves technical unknowns, market changes, shifting customer expectations, and dependencies across teams. Yet roadmaps often extend a year or more into the future with precise dates and scope. The further a plan reaches, the less reliable it becomes. Teams then spend energy defending outdated commitments instead of responding to new information. This creates a cycle of missed deadlines, rushed releases, and declining trust.\n\nOrganizational incentives make matters worse. Sales teams may seek features to close deals. Executives may want visible launches. Engineering may prefer work that reduces technical risk. Marketing may need fixed dates for campaigns. Each perspective is reasonable, but a roadmap becomes ineffective when it is simply a negotiated list of requests. Prioritization requires hard trade-offs based on strategy, customer value, effort, and evidence—not political influence.\n\nFinally, many roadmaps fail because they are not revisited often enough. A roadmap should be a living decision-making tool, updated as teams learn. It should include clear goals, assumptions, measures of success, and room for experimentation. When evidence changes, the roadmap should change too.\n\nThe best roadmaps are not rigid predictions of the future. They are disciplined, transparent statements of intent. They connect strategy to customer problems, measure outcomes rather than activity, and give teams permission to learn. In uncertain markets, adaptability is not a weakness in a roadmap; it is the reason a roadmap can succeed.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Most product roadmaps fail because they confuse planning with prediction, outputs with outcomes, and internal politics with customer value.**\n\nA product roadmap is meant to communicate direction: what problems the team will solve, roughly when, and why it matters. In practice, many become Gantt charts of features with dates, treated as contracts. Stakeholders treat dates as promises; teams treat them as commitments. Reality—new customer evidence, competitive moves, technical surprises, or capacity limits—then turns the document into a source of missed expectations and eroded trust. The failure is not that plans change; it is that the organization never treated the roadmap as a hypothesis that would be revised.\n\nA second common failure is output-obsession. Roadmaps list features (“redesign checkout,” “add AI search”) rather than outcomes (“reduce cart abandonment by X,” “increase successful searches among new users”). Without a clear problem and success metric, teams ship work that looks busy but does not move the business. Discovery is often thin or after-the-fact: personas, interviews, and experiments happen too late or not at all, so the roadmap encodes assumptions instead of evidence. Prioritization then defaults to the loudest voice, the biggest customer, or the next quarter’s demo rather than impact versus effort.\n\nAlignment is equally fragile. Engineering, design, sales, support, and leadership often do not share the same understanding of strategy or constraints. A roadmap that is not co-created or regularly socialized becomes a private artifact of the product team. Dependencies, technical debt, and capacity are ignored or underestimated, producing an overcommitted plan. When the plan inevitably slips, blame replaces learning.\n\nFinally, many roadmaps are static. Markets, users, and technology do not wait for the next annual planning cycle. Teams that cannot re-prioritize without drama, or that lack a lightweight “now / next / later” structure, either freeze in place or thrash. The document stops being a decision tool and becomes theater.\n\nRoadmaps that work are shorter on dates and longer on problems, evidence, and intended outcomes. They are living, visible, and revised as learning arrives. They connect strategy to sequenced bets rather than to a feature factory. Most fail not because planning is useless, but because organizations use the artifact to avoid the harder work of discovery, trade-offs, and honest communication.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
